  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about baseline performance, data quality, or which design axes matter. The most critical unknown is whether a simple RNA-only model with a standard PCA embedding achieves reasonable validation accuracy, establishing the performance floor for the entire search. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the simplest, most interpretable configuration in the space. It isolates the contribution of a basic RNA representation without confounding factors from multiprofile aggregation, learned encoders, image modalities, or fusion strategies. It answers: 'What is the performance ceiling of the simplest viable model?' 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: Establishing this baseline is a prerequisite for interpreting every subsequent experiment. Without it, a multimodal or multiprofile result cannot be attributed to the added complexity versus the data itself. PCA is also computationally cheap, reducing the risk of wasting budget on a pipeline failure. This single experiment maximally reduces uncertainty about the starting point and provides a concrete reference against which all later gains (from scvi/scgpt encoders, multiprofile aggregation, image modality, or fusion) can be measured."
